Vendored ACPICA headers, acpica-unix-20260408
=============================================

Why this exists
---------------

arch/all-pc/kernel needs these to compile. include/acpica.h next to this
directory is staged as libraries/acpica.h and includes eight of them
(actypes.h, actbl.h, acbuffer.h, acrestyp.h, acpixf.h, acexcep.h, aclocal.h,
acobject.h), and four kernel sources include that wrapper:

    arch/all-pc/kernel/{acpi,acpi_pm,apic_ia32,platform_post}.c

Without them kernel-kernel does not build, and nothing downstream of the kernel
builds either:

    GENINCDIR/libraries/acpica.h:47:10:
        fatal error: 'acpica/actypes.h' file not found

The headers normally arrive with the fetched ACPICA Port, staged by
acpica-includes-copy in ../../mmakefile.src, which hangs on ports-includes. The
relocatable toolchain producer builds offline from a verified source set and
deliberately keeps ports-includes out of its closure, so that route is not
available there. The same applies to a plain CMake configure, which does not
fetch Ports.

Provenance
----------

source/include from acpica-unix-20260408, with
../../acpica-unix-20260408-aros.diff applied, which is the form the build uses.
The archive comes from Intel's download mirror and axrt.org; ../../mmakefile.src
records the identifiers. Taken from an extracted Ports tree rather than
re-fetched, because Intel's mirror answers 403 for a plain request.

Whole directory, 64 files including platform/. The closure actually reachable
from the AROS wrapper is 12 headers, but pruning to those would need redoing
whenever a kernel source starts using another part of the API.

This is headers only. The ACPICA implementation is still built from the Port, by
kernel-acpica-sharedlib, which needs the sources this directory does not carry.

Updating
--------

Bump ACPICAVERSION in ../../mmakefile.src, refresh the AROS patch, and replace
this directory from source/include of the patched tree. Then check that every
header ../acpica.h includes still resolves here.

Licence
-------

Intel's ACPICA dual licence, reproduced in full at the top of every file. No
separate licence file accompanies source/include upstream.
